"""Data-driven report and static publication figures, without new model selection."""
import numpy as np,pandas as pd,json

def generate(out,lock,c,summary,families,pe,focal,mcs,spa,non):
 def table(f):return f.to_markdown(index=False,floatfmt='.4f') if len(f) else 'None.'
 def rows(f,cols):return table(f[cols])
 positives=families['B'][families['B'].positive_directional_support];negative=families['B'][families['B'].Association=='significant negative association'];wins=focal[focal.Model_win_after_Holm]
 reduced=non[(non.Test=='DM')&(non.Benchmark=='Persistence')];favorable=c[(c.PT_positive_support)|c.DM_win_vs_Persistence|c.DM_win_vs_200DMA|c.DM_win_vs_Momentum60]
 lines=['# JOF Phase3 final statistical inference','This analysis uses only frozen saved predictions. No model was trained or tuned. The final universe contains six neural architectures, two other learned models and five deterministic benchmarks. All family memberships, test definitions and bootstrap settings were fixed before calculations.','## Core interpretation','Primary loss is Brier. For d=loss_model-loss_benchmark, negative favors the learned model and positive favors the benchmark. Probabilistic learned-model loss uses the equal-weight mean of five saved seed probabilities; directional statistics use the unchanged majority labels. These evaluate different forecast objects. Rule probabilities are their deterministic0/1 outputs, so Brier comparisons can favor softer probability forecasts without demonstrating directional superiority.','All conventional full-sample tests have an H20 overlapping-label caveat. HAC19 and stationary blocks with expected length20 are prespecified approximations, not proof that all dependence is removed.','Reduced-overlap evaluation lowers mechanical label overlap but does not establish statistical independence and substantially reduces sample size.','## Fourteen research questions',
 '1. Learned models with positive Holm40-adjusted PT support:\n\n'+rows(positives,['Market','Model','PT_stat','raw_p','Holm40_p','Forecast_Up_Rate'])+'\n\nNeural-only Holm30 support:\n\n'+rows(families['A'][families['A'].positive_directional_support],['Market','Model','Holm30_p']),
 '2. Positive signals by model across five markets:\n\n'+rows(summary,['Model','Positive_PT_raw','Positive_PT_Holm40','Positive_PT_Holm30'])+'\n\nAn isolated positive exception is not stable cross-market evidence. Significant negative association is reported separately and never counted as skill.',
 '3. Brier wins against Persistence after the fixed40-test Holm family:\n\n'+rows(wins[wins.Model_B=='Persistence'],['Market','Model_A','Mean_differential','Benchmark_family_Holm_p']),
 '4. Brier wins against200DMA after its separate40-test Holm family:\n\n'+rows(wins[wins.Model_B=='Rule_200DMA'],['Market','Model_A','Mean_differential','Benchmark_family_Holm_p']),
 '5. Brier wins against Momentum60 after its separate40-test Holm family:\n\n'+rows(wins[wins.Model_B=='Momentum60'],['Market','Model_A','Mean_differential','Benchmark_family_Holm_p']),
 '6. Original Transformer vs PE-Transformer: positive mean differential favors PE, negative favors original. Architecture-sensitive differences use the prespecified five-market DM Holm family; isolated significance is not cross-market superiority.\n\n'+rows(pe,['Market','Mean_differential','raw_two_sided_p','Holm5_p','Favors_PE']),
 '7. CfC vs LTC: the fixed benchmark/PT results for each are below. This is descriptive of inferential advantage, with no additional outcome-selected CfC-vs-LTC family.\n\n'+rows(c[c.Model.isin(['CfC','LNN'])],['Market','Model','BA','PT_Holm40','DM_vs_Persistence_Holm','DM_vs_Persistence_mean_differential']),
 '8. MCS membership (Brier, eleven fixed candidates):\n\n'+table(pd.read_csv(out/'model_confidence_sets_by_market.csv'))+'\n\nThe MCS procedure did not eliminate these models at the stated confidence level. Retention does not establish equality or identify a winner set.',
 '9. SPA against Persistence:\n\n'+rows(spa,['Market','SPA_statistic','p_value','Reject_at_0_05','Best_mean_improvement_model'])+'\n\nSPA tests whether the benchmark can be rejected against the set of alternatives. Rejection does not identify every superior alternative; the best mean differential model is descriptive.',
 '10. Reduced-overlap primary Persistence contrasts (raw small-sample diagnostics):\n\n'+rows(reduced,['Market','Model','N','Mean_differential','raw_p','Status'])+'\n\nLoss of significance can reflect reduced power and dependence sensitivity; it does not alone prove an original result was invalid. Descriptive reduced-overlap MCS was not run because all N are below the prespecified100-observation minimum.',
 '11. Statistically favorable models and frequency warnings:\n\n'+rows(favorable,['Market','Model','Forecast_Up_Rate','near_constant_warning'])+'\n\nA near-constant classification can coexist with varying probabilities. Neither is removed or retuned.',
 '12. Economics of statistically favorable forecasts (existing10bps accounting):\n\n'+rows(favorable,['Market','Model','Economic_CAGR_10bps','Sharpe_10bps','Exposure','Turnover_10bps','exposure_warning'])+'\n\nPredictive-loss advantages do not establish economic advantages. No economic significance test is added here; extreme exposure may reflect persistent exposure or market avoidance.',
 '13. Cross-market summary:\n\n'+table(summary)+'\n\nNo stable cross-market dominance is established by this table. Ranks are descriptive; raw conditional GW rejections alone are not superiority claims.',
 '14. The evidence must be read jointly as benchmark-sensitive, architecture-sensitive, dependence-sensitive and multiplicity-sensitive. Isolated favorable findings are statistically fragile rather than universal superiority. The design thesis is assessed through the changes across fixed diagnostics below, not by treating every significant raw p-value as support.\n\n'+table(c.groupby('Final_assessment').size().rename('Rows').reset_index()),
 '## Significant negative PT associations',rows(negative,['Market','Model','PT_stat','Holm40_p','Forecast_Up_Rate']),
 '## Inference limitations','Only five market blocks exist, learned configurations were validation-selected, and all diagnostics reuse the same test observations. They are not independent confirmations. Original training is expanding rather than the fixed rolling window assumed in some GW theory; our reported GW-style conditional moment Wald test is a precisely specified robustness diagnostic, not exact original GW. It conditions on resolved historical losses, so its sample starts later. Monthly regimes were not refit. Log loss is secondary and learned-only, with unadjusted pairwise results stored without superiority claims. Bootstrap p-values are Monte Carlo approximations conditional on a fixed expected block length20; block-length sensitivity is not searched.']
 (out/'results_report.md').write_text('\n\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
 (out/'methodology_notes.md').write_text('''# Methodology

Primary differential: d_t = loss_A,t - loss_B,t. Negative favors A; positive favors B. For focal comparisons A is the learned model, B the frozen benchmark. No sign reversal is permitted. Brier uses equal-weight arithmetic mean saved five-seed probabilities for learned models and0/1 probabilities for rules. PT, BA and MacroF1 use binary3-of-5 majority, with deterministic consensus verified for logistic andLightGBM. Neither labels nor probability averaging are tuned. Source predictions, selected losses and thresholds remain unchanged.

DM: sample mean differential divided by sqrt(Bartlett Newey-West long-run variance/N), lag19, biased autocovariances denominatorN. HLN factor sqrt((N+1-2H+H(H-1)/N)/N),H20, applied uniformly; two-sided Student t reference dfN-1. Zero/invalid variance yields undefined status, never a manufactured p-value. All78 Brier pairs and28 secondary learned-only logloss pairs per market are stored. Logloss eps1e-12. Three benchmark-specific40-test focal Holm families are separate; PE sensitivity is a separate5-test family. Raw full pairwise results are not an invitation to unadjusted favorable pair selection.

GW-style conditional moment Wald: g_t=z_t*d_t, z=(1,most_recent_resolved_d,previous_raw_abs_LogRet,previous_raw_Vol20). A loss for target j resolves at j+20; it is usable only when that date is no later than target t-1. Thus naive d[t-1] is forbidden. Missing unresolved early rows are excluded uniformly, and an availability assertion is applied. Instruments are rescaled by fixed1,1,.01,.01 for numerical conditioning; this invertible transformation leaves Wald unchanged. Statistic N*gbar' S_HAC^-1*gbar,chi-square4df,lag19. Singular covariance returns undefined. This is not claimed as exact original GW: original rolling forecast-estimation assumptions are not met by saved expanding-fit forecasts. A significant result tests equality of conditional moments, not one model's superiority; consult mean loss differential for direction.

PT: unchanged previous validated Pesaran–Timmermann implementation, conventional two-sided p-values. Positive support requires positive statistic and adjusted p<.05. Significant negative association never counts as positive skill. Undefined constant-forecast PT retains originalNaN, while an input1 placeholder conservatively preserves all planned Holm family sizes; adjusted result remainsNaN and supportFalse. A30 neural, B40 learned, C5 LTC, D5 paired PE DM are fixed in protocol_lock.json. Family B is the broad primary classification in the conclusion matrix; family A is additionally reported explicitly for neural models. No prior Holm output is overwritten.

MCS: arch8.0.0 MCS rangeR statistic, stationary joint-row bootstrap, expected block length20,10000 replicates,seed20261106. All eleven predefined candidates are retained even if thresholded learned majority is constant; only prespecified Always-Up/Down are outside MCS. One elimination path supplies monotone MCS p-values and both90%/95% membership. P>.10 means retained at90%;p>.05 at95%. Elimination order is recorded, including the final survivor with p1. Retention does not mean equivalent expected losses.

SPA: Hansen studentized maximum of positive standardized mean improvements, Persistence minus alternative loss. Joint stationary-bootstrap means supply long-run variances N*var(bootstrap_mean). Consistent recentering keeps means not below -sigma/sqrtN*sqrt(2loglogN), otherwise zero. Observed and bootstrap maxima include zero. P=(1+count(bootstrap_stat>=observed))/(10001). This corrects for the fixed ten-alternative search within each market; no additional across-market SPA Holm family was requested. All ten alternatives remain. Implementation is independently compared against arch8 SPA on identically standardized data and common draws. The arch8 studentize flag by itself did not scale simulated differentials in the inspected source, so the explicit verified studentized implementation is used and not silently mislabeled. Source hashes are archived.

Reduced overlap: fixed phase0,step20 saved prediction rows. Effective forecast stepH1 gives DM HAC0/HLNH1, prespecified; full data always HAC19/H20. Approximately47-49 observations imply low power. PT and focal reduced DM are raw diagnostics, not newly corrected positive-skill claims. MCS not run below100 per fixed rule. Reduced-overlap evaluation lowers mechanical label overlap but does not establish statistical independence and substantially reduces sample size.

Economic columns copy the existing10bps long/cash results without new assumptions or economic tests. Extreme exposure<.1/>.9 and forecast-up<.1/>.9 are flags, never exclusions. Conclusion categories follow the frozen rule, are descriptive and never label a model robust based on correlated diagnostics. Focal DM wins require mean d<0 and family Holm<.05; significant d>0 favors benchmark. The same market days underlie probability and classification metrics but those forecast objects differ; Brier can favor softened forecasts over deterministic extremes even when BA is weak.
''')
 import matplotlib
 matplotlib.use('Agg')
 import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
 plt.rcParams.update({'font.size':9,'savefig.dpi':300,'figure.dpi':120})
 for name,title,values,vmin,vmax,cmap in [
  ('pt_holm40','PT Holm40: -log10(p); sign annotated',-np.log10(c.pivot(index='Model',columns='Market',values='PT_Holm40').clip(lower=1e-12)),0,4,'viridis'),
  ('mcs90_membership','MCS90 retention (1=not eliminated)',c.pivot(index='Model',columns='Market',values='MCS90_member').astype(float),0,1,'Blues'),
  ('dm_benchmark_wins','Holm-adjusted Brier wins vs Persistence',c.pivot(index='Model',columns='Market',values='DM_win_vs_Persistence').astype(float),0,1,'Greens')]:
  values=values.reindex(index=lock['learned'],columns=lock['markets']);fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(8,4.5),layout='constrained');im=ax.imshow(values.to_numpy(),vmin=vmin,vmax=vmax,cmap=cmap,aspect='auto');ax.set_xticks(range(5),values.columns,rotation=25,ha='right');ax.set_yticks(range(8),values.index);ax.set_title(title)
  for i,m in enumerate(values.index):
   for j,market in enumerate(values.columns):
    row=c[(c.Model==m)&(c.Market==market)].iloc[0];v=values.iloc[i,j];label=('NA' if not np.isfinite(v) else f'{row.PT_Holm40:.3f}'+(' +' if row.PT_stat>0 else ' -')) if name=='pt_holm40' else str(int(v));ax.text(j,i,label,ha='center',va='center',fontsize=8,color='black')
  fig.colorbar(im,ax=ax,shrink=.8);fig.savefig(out/'figures'/f'{name}.png');fig.savefig(out/'figures'/f'{name}.pdf');plt.close(fig)
